[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-342074"] :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x(x-2){y}^{′}-4y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 2x(x-2){y}^{′} & =(2{x}^{2}-4x)\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -4y & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the ${y}^{′}$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n2x(x-2){y}^{′}={\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +m-1){a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(x-2){y}^{′} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-4\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & =-4\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[2(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-4(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -4y & =-4{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-4\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-4{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -4\\lambda -4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -4){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -4){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -4){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-345272/selection/4c8fa12a615b7538c57226d12b92deb4552b33b1c914a5901356d69ca9f27c1e" :answer/id #uuid "81977c79-4114-5a54-b113-8b4dea52eb18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{\\lambda }^{2}+9\\lambda +3=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345272/selection/b2e30a9891415e09f63aeb6226499a6780204e47b22f5c253b8c9d6fffdbb03a" :answer/id #uuid "b20ab308-6b2f-5307-8f30-fc6cd94d8c18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda -3=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345272/selection/82b2eefcbcc246df63758cfa92eb6d20a2b302d23910ccdb1f681f7e385d95af" :answer/id #uuid "95fda030-bac2-50fa-b61c-4389689e6f8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345272/selection/0d571b7161ce99cf5b1efcaed6f10a688b1aa3738f6b144f9c3b8fe269b227f1" :answer/id #uuid "7bcd87ec-7a52-522f-8f5d-53562c1fc36e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345272/selection/24b679758888db4d5fe9e94494af007711877564649cae42043e799cf66da7b4" :answer/id #uuid "4a1be3de-d75a-506b-93ed-db4b1ea9dffd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +3=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345272/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf98d2dd-a291-5ddf-aecf-f5b17b7d58a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-345272/selection/0d571b7161ce99cf5b1efcaed6f10a688b1aa3738f6b144f9c3b8fe269b227f1" :answer-field/choices ["q-345272/selection/4c8fa12a615b7538c57226d12b92deb4552b33b1c914a5901356d69ca9f27c1e" "q-345272/selection/b2e30a9891415e09f63aeb6226499a6780204e47b22f5c253b8c9d6fffdbb03a" "q-345272/selection/82b2eefcbcc246df63758cfa92eb6d20a2b302d23910ccdb1f681f7e385d95af" "q-345272/selection/0d571b7161ce99cf5b1efcaed6f10a688b1aa3738f6b144f9c3b8fe269b227f1" "q-345272/selection/24b679758888db4d5fe9e94494af007711877564649cae42043e799cf66da7b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345272" :question/id #uuid "9680cb4a-22e6-56a2-85cb-dba1f58ee479" :question/math-academy-id "q-345272" :question/problem "$$\n4{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+(x+3)y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4{x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -5x{y}^{′} & =-5x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (x+3)y & =(x+3){\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(x+3)y={\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}+3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -5x{y}^{′} & =-5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-5\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5\\lambda +3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-345272/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94dc48ed-1523-5b21-aedb-d920ecd4b8a6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345272"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345183/selection/b1bd4259562a9865a6c16e8ea50a400043153b0a17b816f8b49c0135b5882a21" :answer/id #uuid "e2c38ae3-37e9-52e6-ab5e-50ff4373c513" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda -6=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345183/selection/e66f30f7c26b03022b85ef306549fa485d2c6b904f2e641df64c20679614061f" :answer/id #uuid "aa020fe0-2eb5-5866-880d-cbd6bb4a254c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345183/selection/b3779b095c23cbb12257a41ff42a6ea319477f805acc7773bbc634211e2ecf62" :answer/id #uuid "220e8927-a8e6-5f0d-a8ea-81ead33a299d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345183/selection/60fad6e6584ce461bca159f52dabce72a974e25d2d06b324442ec6b77e122735" :answer/id #uuid "a7554f3d-3b2a-5e19-9e63-7b280765911c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345183/selection/c1e8e02cc70fec1f27d1a2a9b317eab80a379774f14faa1a42d80d32a99b7092" :answer/id #uuid "a01b517e-520b-5c06-bfeb-a946b2a86526" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda -10=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345183/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b00d8c39-e0df-567f-8369-ae487617f0cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-345183/selection/e66f30f7c26b03022b85ef306549fa485d2c6b904f2e641df64c20679614061f" :answer-field/choices ["q-345183/selection/b1bd4259562a9865a6c16e8ea50a400043153b0a17b816f8b49c0135b5882a21" "q-345183/selection/e66f30f7c26b03022b85ef306549fa485d2c6b904f2e641df64c20679614061f" "q-345183/selection/b3779b095c23cbb12257a41ff42a6ea319477f805acc7773bbc634211e2ecf62" "q-345183/selection/60fad6e6584ce461bca159f52dabce72a974e25d2d06b324442ec6b77e122735" "q-345183/selection/c1e8e02cc70fec1f27d1a2a9b317eab80a379774f14faa1a42d80d32a99b7092"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345183" :question/id #uuid "5baa6630-0826-52e7-83b7-68dab414a79e" :question/math-academy-id "q-345183" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x(x-3){y}^{′}-6y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 3x(x-3){y}^{′} & =(3{x}^{2}-9x)\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }9(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -6y & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the ${y}^{′}$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x(x-3){y}^{′}={\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +m-1){a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }9(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(x-3){y}^{′} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }9(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-9\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & =-9\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[3(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-9(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -6y & =-6{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-9\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-6{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -9\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda -6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-345183/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94dc48ed-1523-5b21-aedb-d920ecd4b8a6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345183"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345190/selection/d32ea0999c037b288ba331f33d1f900414fa737e47e9bd35f1bbc3da50f15314" :answer/id #uuid "ba109626-bd52-5340-a88b-ab5f8e4dc819" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345190/selection/54a59a505b6b451cd0c26b412a487437da1b21f60cc1de55b9a7fa3faef8ad5d" :answer/id #uuid "d747c5ff-042f-5c83-94f3-41e470d6292a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-11\\lambda -29=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345190/selection/0efa9e1b5660294c28ced409f5927d4d7cdf9874a7217ff79637dddc7b46dabe" :answer/id #uuid "dd7b8f67-eb6a-50a2-b3ad-1bfcb29e080f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda -11=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345190/selection/ab5b6ced77af2c0510b38c9028383b5a5adbe00e669774358b806925269170d6" :answer/id #uuid "77aba7e7-2080-5695-89a9-b6ce55f5e506" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-28\\lambda +11=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345190/selection/c367d8118082e170e485931a23abb2ca7194bdc3694cd0db59e01894b17d6c58" :answer/id #uuid "ba643473-2efb-5056-9afb-2e74b12fc3a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}+29\\lambda -11=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345190/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "17f07f60-5af7-50e1-90e4-4ddd4bc8d262" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-345190/selection/d32ea0999c037b288ba331f33d1f900414fa737e47e9bd35f1bbc3da50f15314" :answer-field/choices ["q-345190/selection/d32ea0999c037b288ba331f33d1f900414fa737e47e9bd35f1bbc3da50f15314" "q-345190/selection/54a59a505b6b451cd0c26b412a487437da1b21f60cc1de55b9a7fa3faef8ad5d" "q-345190/selection/0efa9e1b5660294c28ced409f5927d4d7cdf9874a7217ff79637dddc7b46dabe" "q-345190/selection/ab5b6ced77af2c0510b38c9028383b5a5adbe00e669774358b806925269170d6" "q-345190/selection/c367d8118082e170e485931a23abb2ca7194bdc3694cd0db59e01894b17d6c58"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345190" :question/id #uuid "8c01952b-2e59-5903-9624-0d9bccd8b57e" :question/math-academy-id "q-345190" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-4x(x+7){y}^{′}+11y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -4x(x+7){y}^{′} & =(-4{x}^{2}-28x)\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }28(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 11y & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }11{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the ${y}^{′}$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-4x(x+7){y}^{′}=-{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +m-1){a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }28(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x(x+7){y}^{′} & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }28(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-28\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }28(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & =-28\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[-4(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-28(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 11y & =11{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }11{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-28\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+11{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -28\\lambda +11){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-29\\lambda +11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-345190/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94dc48ed-1523-5b21-aedb-d920ecd4b8a6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345190"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-342276"] :question/problem "$$\n4x{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{y}^{″} & =4x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 2{y}^{′} & =2\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -y & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $n=m-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-y=-{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n-y=-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{y}^{″} & =4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 2{y}^{′} & =2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +2\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (4{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form ${x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$ for some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ 2(2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficients of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-345399/selection/24c5a7d9cd2421471ad6cf3243bf6efe702880f1c17be337338bf697414d9785" :answer/id #uuid "e4083924-2130-5709-b14a-9058d1b99f89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345399/selection/f7002dc6121b6cd7464780710382da5d91bd21180e8121c46b21bb02ba618ac3" :answer/id #uuid "be4e078a-a164-50c4-9dac-ba86504a9872" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-2/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345399/selection/215d3f2b4e2bad0bf4e9a2cc7653a7eb18e65e94c4bf3bbab158a0ab3db9496b" :answer/id #uuid "9066255f-f55b-5c68-ad25-5f5d9e4be7d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n/2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345399/selection/825eb98e982207c36b1ca19bee1243e0c08685e05bf34f8c4a70231e0929c9ad" :answer/id #uuid "90c6385a-b6c2-54b5-96c5-e51964d274f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345399/selection/d3a54bb7374081320288eae149f9f8bb6f4012b7a27559cb4c3974ca7b9fa8eb" :answer/id #uuid "45f68074-dab8-50d9-afef-d86adff0e654" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345399/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d84d9c65-e5e9-5422-b9e3-8bb6692d65ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-345399/selection/d3a54bb7374081320288eae149f9f8bb6f4012b7a27559cb4c3974ca7b9fa8eb" :answer-field/choices ["q-345399/selection/24c5a7d9cd2421471ad6cf3243bf6efe702880f1c17be337338bf697414d9785" "q-345399/selection/f7002dc6121b6cd7464780710382da5d91bd21180e8121c46b21bb02ba618ac3" "q-345399/selection/215d3f2b4e2bad0bf4e9a2cc7653a7eb18e65e94c4bf3bbab158a0ab3db9496b" "q-345399/selection/825eb98e982207c36b1ca19bee1243e0c08685e05bf34f8c4a70231e0929c9ad" "q-345399/selection/d3a54bb7374081320288eae149f9f8bb6f4012b7a27559cb4c3974ca7b9fa8eb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345399" :question/id #uuid "4957a26c-b805-56c7-a4e7-6ccb54bd1f88" :question/math-academy-id "q-345399" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x(6x-1){y}^{′}-3y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation given above, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+6{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x(6x-1){y}^{′} & =-\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -3y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+6{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x(6x-1){y}^{′} & =-\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -3y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda -\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution.\nSolving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (3\\lambda +1)(2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}_{2}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-345399/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5d6ee-cc3b-5473-8cfc-0ccecd631449"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345399"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345279/selection/0f903ec07c20fd2a3198b9f6c43a0256c78e7434291b7925ad860f5be0f03bcf" :answer/id #uuid "d49ebba5-349e-5aac-bdac-e18b84acacec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345279/selection/780e1ca34c143672fdea94b4ec5ce0b161ed8cf7a561144b6f2e209127e40ed0" :answer/id #uuid "7fabc981-6bc5-59d8-b218-3a27eefa4fb9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+2/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345279/selection/4261e56c75bebbabf8058d2d57d40b7e853e7efc70bfe4ac4d9e443ec6b0b638" :answer/id #uuid "014db5c0-f435-5451-be85-46ab6acc1bc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345279/selection/24c5a7d9cd2421471ad6cf3243bf6efe702880f1c17be337338bf697414d9785" :answer/id #uuid "11b0f5ce-56ca-58ed-93db-244399617f67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345279/selection/8c5dbfb2b6925f1b1f5016c7c3c1d682de352150e6ca68aa3b010735e2af77ce" :answer/id #uuid "6c6331e1-bcbf-57f6-b3fd-980e906b6121" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345279/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "999533e4-8880-595e-bf32-63627d1a2a18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-345279/selection/24c5a7d9cd2421471ad6cf3243bf6efe702880f1c17be337338bf697414d9785" :answer-field/choices ["q-345279/selection/0f903ec07c20fd2a3198b9f6c43a0256c78e7434291b7925ad860f5be0f03bcf" "q-345279/selection/780e1ca34c143672fdea94b4ec5ce0b161ed8cf7a561144b6f2e209127e40ed0" "q-345279/selection/4261e56c75bebbabf8058d2d57d40b7e853e7efc70bfe4ac4d9e443ec6b0b638" "q-345279/selection/24c5a7d9cd2421471ad6cf3243bf6efe702880f1c17be337338bf697414d9785" "q-345279/selection/8c5dbfb2b6925f1b1f5016c7c3c1d682de352150e6ca68aa3b010735e2af77ce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345279" :question/id #uuid "b92a43ef-d730-5ffc-8eed-ac697171f23b" :question/math-academy-id "q-345279" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+12x{y}^{′}+(9x-2)y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =9({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 12x{y}^{′} & =12\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }12(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (9x-2)y & =-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(9{a}_{n-1}-2{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =9({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 12x{y}^{′} & =12\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }12(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (9x-2)y & =-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(9{a}_{n-1}-2{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+12\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (9{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +12\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (3\\lambda -1)(3\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-2/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-345279/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5d6ee-cc3b-5473-8cfc-0ccecd631449"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345279"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345430/selection/788aa4255e5eb5bbcd591978f783bd939c9fa854efc9572e4bb8122ac05788db" :answer/id #uuid "2cc63f18-74c3-540a-a6b5-adf5f44efaa2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-3/10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345430/selection/fd6a8ed634109dceaf44ec7cfc9e41e1e2e3449745c2184919665c58fd1b17da" :answer/id #uuid "95462876-c7af-5c3c-92d4-f63467fefd93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345430/selection/395587249e12848c3a3433643ff1169216b2e74c5d06cf7625b2c7ea91849e21" :answer/id #uuid "c4a326cc-e547-51bf-ac68-1c5c28c97942" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-3/5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345430/selection/237cc35bda5176b0c1c44552069d6326d09467392d2417a7c16652e2836a7e4f" :answer/id #uuid "9c17eb15-a9ec-5742-b9d6-d377efa68682" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345430/selection/e8c0e6efbae0dbf54504ca2c61027a6b98890e62d606fa7a6b62147c1395a039" :answer/id #uuid "aca2c04f-635e-50bd-a839-85a2cd13ffce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345430/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "82f58078-9047-5de7-92f0-ad19cd0a4c79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-345430/selection/fd6a8ed634109dceaf44ec7cfc9e41e1e2e3449745c2184919665c58fd1b17da" :answer-field/choices ["q-345430/selection/788aa4255e5eb5bbcd591978f783bd939c9fa854efc9572e4bb8122ac05788db" "q-345430/selection/fd6a8ed634109dceaf44ec7cfc9e41e1e2e3449745c2184919665c58fd1b17da" "q-345430/selection/395587249e12848c3a3433643ff1169216b2e74c5d06cf7625b2c7ea91849e21" "q-345430/selection/237cc35bda5176b0c1c44552069d6326d09467392d2417a7c16652e2836a7e4f" "q-345430/selection/e8c0e6efbae0dbf54504ca2c61027a6b98890e62d606fa7a6b62147c1395a039"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345430" :question/id #uuid "127ad728-6af7-5698-a09b-a69e783ab7ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-345430" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n10x{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}-4y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x{y}^{″} & =10({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 7{y}^{′} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -4y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-4{a}_{n-1}){x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x{y}^{″} & =10({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 7{y}^{′} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -4y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-4{a}_{n-1}){x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (10{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +7\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (10\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/10},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/10}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-345430/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5d6ee-cc3b-5473-8cfc-0ccecd631449"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345430"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345432/selection/8c5dbfb2b6925f1b1f5016c7c3c1d682de352150e6ca68aa3b010735e2af77ce" :answer/id #uuid "5c20a31b-c899-562e-8bd7-be0866e59166" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345432/selection/215d3f2b4e2bad0bf4e9a2cc7653a7eb18e65e94c4bf3bbab158a0ab3db9496b" :answer/id #uuid "830deb66-f6db-5bfb-b439-cddd5593b53c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n/2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345432/selection/4261e56c75bebbabf8058d2d57d40b7e853e7efc70bfe4ac4d9e443ec6b0b638" :answer/id #uuid "b5e4a637-e56b-5c40-9731-d526e6b1b535" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345432/selection/0f903ec07c20fd2a3198b9f6c43a0256c78e7434291b7925ad860f5be0f03bcf" :answer/id #uuid "79d15c46-3335-54d1-9011-5f47a699e758" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345432/selection/780e1ca34c143672fdea94b4ec5ce0b161ed8cf7a561144b6f2e209127e40ed0" :answer/id #uuid "b2421d79-3162-58d5-8d59-4d4883f4db84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+2/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345432/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "df64f0b3-49af-552b-9739-e8ef5776a0b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-345432/selection/8c5dbfb2b6925f1b1f5016c7c3c1d682de352150e6ca68aa3b010735e2af77ce" :answer-field/choices ["q-345432/selection/8c5dbfb2b6925f1b1f5016c7c3c1d682de352150e6ca68aa3b010735e2af77ce" "q-345432/selection/215d3f2b4e2bad0bf4e9a2cc7653a7eb18e65e94c4bf3bbab158a0ab3db9496b" "q-345432/selection/4261e56c75bebbabf8058d2d57d40b7e853e7efc70bfe4ac4d9e443ec6b0b638" "q-345432/selection/0f903ec07c20fd2a3198b9f6c43a0256c78e7434291b7925ad860f5be0f03bcf" "q-345432/selection/780e1ca34c143672fdea94b4ec5ce0b161ed8cf7a561144b6f2e209127e40ed0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345432" :question/id #uuid "80abd8ea-e468-5c87-8eeb-1d8a6240d099" :question/math-academy-id "q-345432" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x(6x+1){y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation given above, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+6{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x(6x+1){y}^{′} & =\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}+(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ y & ={a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+6{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x(6x+1){y}^{′} & =\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}+(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ y & ={a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution.\nSolving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (3\\lambda -1)(2\\lambda -1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}_{2}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-345432/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5d6ee-cc3b-5473-8cfc-0ccecd631449"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345432"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345407/selection/f7002dc6121b6cd7464780710382da5d91bd21180e8121c46b21bb02ba618ac3" :answer/id #uuid "4303118b-4026-5fe5-8176-66f5097d5917" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-2/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345407/selection/cec6f7bc6eda4f461e30e8d49917ae73dea812db8c30209db3f5e78bd0f5d309" :answer/id #uuid "173c81a8-1fc1-539f-86de-a0bddea20f0b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+5/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345407/selection/780e1ca34c143672fdea94b4ec5ce0b161ed8cf7a561144b6f2e209127e40ed0" :answer/id #uuid "bb149880-2b80-5074-a87b-99a6d7644027" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+2/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345407/selection/fe88d0886fdd1f10456b59f004e10ba79f9a64e0269e5c34af9c69b51e34e6e7" :answer/id #uuid "624c4310-489f-5be4-af73-6f16e4009323" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-5/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345407/selection/24c5a7d9cd2421471ad6cf3243bf6efe702880f1c17be337338bf697414d9785" :answer/id #uuid "9be4f965-a3b6-55ef-a171-75411c269cb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345407/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "09ffae29-05a4-5865-8930-9a438f0e959d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-345407/selection/cec6f7bc6eda4f461e30e8d49917ae73dea812db8c30209db3f5e78bd0f5d309" :answer-field/choices ["q-345407/selection/f7002dc6121b6cd7464780710382da5d91bd21180e8121c46b21bb02ba618ac3" "q-345407/selection/cec6f7bc6eda4f461e30e8d49917ae73dea812db8c30209db3f5e78bd0f5d309" "q-345407/selection/780e1ca34c143672fdea94b4ec5ce0b161ed8cf7a561144b6f2e209127e40ed0" "q-345407/selection/fe88d0886fdd1f10456b59f004e10ba79f9a64e0269e5c34af9c69b51e34e6e7" "q-345407/selection/24c5a7d9cd2421471ad6cf3243bf6efe702880f1c17be337338bf697414d9785"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345407" :question/id #uuid "603b845d-4471-5a0a-9912-45cae4b99e65" :question/math-academy-id "q-345407" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n6x{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+3y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation, where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -4{y}^{′} & =-4\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-4(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 3y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(3{a}_{n-1}){x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x{y}^{″} & =6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ -4{y}^{′} & =-4\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-4(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ 3y & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(3{a}_{n-1}){x}^{\\lambda +n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}-4\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda -4\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & = \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda -1}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda ){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (3\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =0,\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+5/3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${a}_{n}$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases.\n\nOf the given options, the correct answer is\n$y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+5/3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-345407/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a5d6ee-cc3b-5473-8cfc-0ccecd631449"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345407"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21851"] :question/problem "$$\nx^2y'' + x(x-3)y' - 6y = 0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ and $a_n$ for $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary $a_0,$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n \\geq 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n\n= \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^{\\lambda+n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives $y'$ and $y''{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1})}^{′}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series in the equation so that they use the same index of summation, the powers of $x$ are the same, and the indices start at the same value.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ x(x-3){y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}-3x)\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -6y & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y'$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(x-3)y'\n=\n\\sum_{m=1}^\\infty (\\lambda+m-1)a_{m-1}x^{\\lambda+m}\n-\n\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty 3(\\lambda+n)a_nx^{\\lambda+n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the ${\\color{blue}n=0}$ term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-3){y}^{′} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-3\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & =-3\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(\\lambda +n-1){a}_{n-1}-3(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the ${\\color{blue}n=0}$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ -6y & =-6{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for ${\\color{blue}n=0},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-6{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -3\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\geq 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n$\\displaystyle\nx^{\\lambda}\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(\\lambda)x^n,$\nfor some $b_n(\\lambda),$ $n\\geq 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary $a_0\\neq0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21897"] :question/problem "$$\n12x^2y'' + 7xy' + (2x-3)y = 0\n$$\n\nFind the possible values of $\\lambda$ for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above, and write the corresponding Frobenius series forms.\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =12({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }12((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 7x{y}^{′} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (2x-3)y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2{a}_{n-1}-3{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny = x^\\lambda \\sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_nx^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ and $a_n$ for $n=0,1,2,\\ldots$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain:\n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary $a_0,$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n \\geq 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $\\color{blue}n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =12({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }12((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 7x{y}^{′} & =7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (2x-3)y & =-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2{a}_{n-1}-3{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for ${\\color{blue}n=0},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+7\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-3{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (12{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +7\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\geq 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n$\\displaystyle\nx^{\\lambda}\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(\\lambda)x^n,$\nfor some $b_n(\\lambda),$ $n \\geq 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary $a_0\\neq0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine the possible values of $\\lambda.$ Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (4\\lambda -3)(3\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the two Frobenius series solutions have the forms\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,y(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+3/4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the constants $a_n$ are to be determined separately in each of the two cases." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
